{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '54,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"    54\t\\section{Related Work}\n    55\t\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and compression.}\n    56\tMMLU, MTEB, and HELM illustrate the breadth and heterogeneity of model evaluation \\citep{MMLU,MTEB,HELM}. Their roles differ: MTEB evaluates embeddings, whereas the other settings concern language-model capabilities more broadly. Reducing evaluation within a benchmark, as in tinyBenchmarks \\citep{Tiny}, is distinct from purchasing complete benchmark scores. Metabench uses sparse item-level measurements to estimate underlying benchmark abilities \\citep{Metabench}; our observational unit is instead an already aggregated model--benchmark score. BenchBench emphasizes that agreement conclusions depend on evaluation choices \\citep{BenchBench}. We accordingly state the precise prediction target rather than treating correlation or a high aggregate score as sufficient validation.\n    57\t\n    58\t\\paragraph{Latent structure and experimental design.}\n    59\tObservational scaling laws and Sloth motivate low-dimensional descriptions of multi-benchmark performance \\citep{Observational,Sloth}. We do not fit compute scaling laws or recover interpretable skills. Our factors are regularized predictive coordinates, following the broader matrix-factorization tradition \\citep{Koren,PMF,BayesianMF}. Masked numerical ratings should not be confused with implicit positive/negative feedback \\citep{Hu}. Matrix completion has both convex and alternating-minimization approaches \\citep{CandesRecht,CandesTao,Keshavan,Mazumder,Jain}; their recovery guarantees require assumptions not verified for these selectively observed leaderboards. Ridge regularization stabilizes the conditional solves \\citep{Hoerl}. The acquisition rule is a prediction-oriented design heuristic, related to Bayesian design and statistical active learning \\citep{Chaloner,Cohn}, not a theorem of absolute-error optimality.\n    60\t\n    61\t\\paragraph{Missingness and uncertainty.}\n    62\tClassical missing-data work distinguishes the observed-data model from assumptions about the observation process \\citep{Rubin,Schafer}. Optimizing a masked loss does not establish that missing leaderboard entries are ignorable, nor is our alternating ridge fit a fitted model of that process or an EM analysis \\citep{Dempster}. Bootstrap inference also depends on the resampling unit \\citep{Efron}. Work on dependent data arrays cautions against treating all cells as independent \\citep{Owen}. Here the supplied interval resamples heuristic families in a paired fashion; it does not quantify every source of distributional or implementation uncertainty.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\section{Method}\n    65\t\\label{sec:method}\n    66\t\\subsection{Problem and success criterion}\n    67\tLet $X\\in[0,1]^{M\\times N}$ be historical scores, with observed-entry mask $W$. For a held-out model, let $A$ denote the available benchmark indices, $Q_k\\subset A$ the purchased indices after budget $k$, and $x_j$ the measured score at index $j$. The predictor receives only $X,W,A$, and purchased values. It outputs $\\hat x_j$ for the remaining targets $R_k=A\\setminus Q_k$. For one held-out row, the reported normalized prediction score is\n    68\t\\begin{equation}\n    69\t S_k=1-\\frac{1}{|R_k|}\\sum_{j\\in R_k}\n    70\t \\left|\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}(\\hat x_j)-x_j\\right|.\n    71\t \\label{eq:score}\n    72\t\\end{equation}\n    73\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#curve_score]]\n    74\tHigher is better. This is not classification accuracy, explained variance, or a fraction of recovered benchmarks. Each benchmark purchase has equal nominal cost; the objective does not incorporate measured benchmark runtimes. Missing historical cells contribute no observations, even though the transport representation stores zeros in those positions. The new model's availability mask is supplied before selection, which is more information than an evaluator may have in a prospective deployment.\n    75\t\n    76\t\\subsection{Masked factors and conditional prediction}\n    77\tFor benchmark $j$, compute the historical observed mean $\\mu_j$. With latent row coordinates $u_i\\in\\mathbb R^r$ and benchmark factors $v_j\\in\\mathbb R^r$, the fitted objective is\n    78\t\\begin{equation}\n    79\t \\min_{U,V}\\sum_{i,j:W_{ij}=1}(X_{ij}-\\mu_j-u_i^\\top v_j)^2\n    80\t       +\\lambda\\left(\\|U\\|_F^2+\\|V\\|_F^2\\right).\n    81\t \\label{eq:fit}\n    82\t\\end{equation}\n    83\tThe means remain fixed during alternating ridge updates. Initialization uses the leading right singular vectors of the zero-filled, mean-centered matrix, scaled by square roots of their singular values. Zero filling is an initialization device, not a claim that missing measurements equal a benchmark mean in the fitted loss.\n    84\t\n    85\tThe regularization coefficient is $\\lambda=0.1$. Rank is chosen from $\\{1,2,4,8\\}$ by absolute error on a deterministic seeded holdout of approximately $10\\%$ of observed historical cells. Infeasible ranks are skipped. Each rank uses at most four alternating sweeps for validation; the chosen rank is refitted on all historical observations for at most eight sweeps, capped by the requested fitting allowance. This is historical-cell validation, not nested validation on held-out model families. The saved results do not report selected ranks, so we do not attribute performance to a particular latent dimensionality.\n    86\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L45-L83]]\n    87\t\n    88\tFor a new model and purchased set $Q$, stack the corresponding factor rows into $V_Q$. Prediction uses\n    89\t\\begin{equation}\n    90\t C_Q=(V_Q^\\top V_Q+\\lambda I)^{-1},\\qquad\n    91\t \\hat u_Q=C_QV_Q^\\top(x_Q-\\mu_Q),\\qquad\n    92\t \\hat x_j=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}(\\mu_j+v_j^\\top\\hat u_Q).\n    93\t \\label{eq:predict}\n    94\t\\end{equation}\n    95\tEach new model has an independent conditional state. Observations from one held-out row do not update another row's latent representation or refit the historical benchmark factors.\n    96\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L110-L142]]\n    97\t\n    98\t\\subsection{Variance-guided benchmark acquisition}\n    99\tLet $T=A\\setminus Q$ be the currently available, unpurchased indices. For each legal next query $a\\in T$, the implemented proxy is\n   100\t\\begin{equation}\n   101\t g(a\\mid Q,A)=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in T}(v_j^\\top C_Qv_a)^2}\n   102\t                         {1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a},\\qquad\n   103\t a^*=\\arg\\max_{a\\in T}g(a\\mid Q,A).\n   104\t \\label{eq:acq}\n   105\t\\end{equation}\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L114-L127]]\n   107\tThe index order resolves ties. Under a unit-noise linear working model, adding $v_a$ changes the inverse information matrix by\n   108\t\\begin{equation}\n   109\t C_{Q\\cup\\{a\\}}=C_Q-\n   110\t \\frac{C_Qv_av_a^\\top C_Q}{1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a}.\n   111\t \\label{eq:rankone}\n   112\t\\end{equation}\n   113\tConsequently, $g$ is the reduction in $\\operatorname{tr}(V_TC_QV_T^\\top)$ \\emph{on the current fixed set $T$} from this update. This identity explains the implementation; it is not a calibrated posterior uncertainty estimate or a guarantee about the measured absolute error.\n   114\t\n   115\tCrucially, the sum includes the queried coordinate $a$. The measured remainder after purchasing $a$ excludes it. Thus the implemented criterion is not pure off-diagonal transfer, and the identity does not establish optimal design for Equation~\\ref{eq:score}. In addition, $C_Q$ depends on purchased indices, not purchased score values. Query sequences can differ between availability masks, but two new models with the same mask follow the same sequence under the same fitted factors. Predictions are response-conditioned; selection is not response-adaptive.\n   116\t\n   117\t\\subsection{Compared policies and computational scope}\n   118\tThe closest comparison, \\textbf{Random LF}, uses the same masked factor-fitting and conditional prediction construction with uniformly random legal queries. \\textbf{Variance LF} denotes the studied selector. The two ridge comparators estimate a benchmark covariance from mean-centered historical scores with missing residuals filled by zero. Their predictor is\n   119\t\\begin{equation}\n   120\t \\hat x=\\mu+\\Sigma_{:,Q}(\\Sigma_{Q,Q}+\\alpha I)^{-1}(x_Q-\\mu_Q),\n   121\t \\label{eq:ridge}\n   122\t\\end{equation}\n   123\tfollowed by clipping. \\textbf{Info ridge} chooses queries by a conditional-covariance reduction criterion; \\textbf{Medoid ridge} orders benchmarks by the sum of absolute imputed historical correlations, skipping unavailable indices. Their penalty is chosen from $\\{0.01,0.1,1\\}$ on every-fifth historical-row validation using a single observed predictor coordinate. They therefore differ from the factor policy in representation, tuning, and potentially query sets; they are not matched-query reconstruction ablations.\n   124\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#run_baseline]]\n   125\t\n   126\t\\textbf{Means} predicts $\\mu$ regardless of observed values. \\textbf{Mean offset} adds the average purchased residual $|Q|^{-1}\\sum_{a\\in Q}(x_a-\\mu_a)$ to each benchmark mean. Both use random legal queries. Even the observation-invariant comparator is scored on its own purchased-set remainder, rather than on an identical untouched panel shared by all policies.\n   127\t\n   128\tThe factor state stores benchmark loadings and a small latent solve instead of a full benchmark covariance. Nevertheless, the inspected code initializes with a dense singular-value decomposition and forms dense products for acquisition. The factor dimension is small, but this implementation is not a sparse-matrix scalability demonstration. We report no measured memory advantage or speedup over ridge. All evaluated methods operate on CPU; no new language-model inference is performed.\n   129\t\n   130\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
