{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '130,251p'\"","aggregated_output":"   130\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n   131\t\\subsection{Public score sources and split construction}\n   132\tWe use two real sources separately. MTEB supplies embedding-model task measurements; Open LLM supplies generative-model scores on IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MUSR, and MMLU-PRO. They are different application regimes, not two subsets of an aligned model roster. The pinned source revisions and extraction rules appear in Supplementary Information. No cross-source entity matching or pooled factor fit is used.\n   133\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#sources]]\n   134\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#build_case]]\n   135\t\n   136\tMTEB extraction uses original test \\texttt{main\\_score} values in their natural unit interval, default/English subsets, and distinct task/revision/subset columns. Open LLM uses raw benchmark columns rather than aggregate or leaderboard-normalized scores. Nonfinite or out-of-bound source values are omitted. No normalization is fitted on held-out scores. Duplicate raw documents and complete score examples are linked before partitioning; normalized identities and aliases are also checked for overlap.\n   137\t\n   138\tWithin each source, the protocol evaluates model-disjoint and heuristic name-family-disjoint splits. Connected identity/content groups are assigned to history or test by seeded group shuffling, with approximately $70\\%$ of groups assigned to history. Family separation adds a conservative name-based linkage. This is stronger than independently splitting rows that may share aliases, but it is not authoritative model genealogy. MTEB columns require at least twenty historical observations, and test rows require enough available values for the full query budget plus a nonempty remainder. Filtering therefore conditions the evaluated population on coverage.\n   139\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#split_components,require_disjoint_rows,build_case]]\n   140\t\n   141\t\\subsection{Budget, weighting, and uncertainty}\n   142\tThe retained final measurements use seeds $1009$, $1013$, and $1019$. MTEB is scored at budgets $k\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}$; Open LLM at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$. Budgets are checkpointed along one query sequence, not independently optimized policies for each $k$. Within a held-out row, target errors have equal weight; rows, checkpoints, seeds, splits within a source, and the two sources are averaged in that order with equal weight at each level. This prevents the larger source or denser target rows from dominating the pooled result.\n   143\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   144\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#aggregation,production]]\n   145\t\n   146\tThe supplied uncertainty interval compares Variance LF with Random LF using $200$ paired family-clustered bootstrap draws and percentile endpoints $2.5$ and $97.5$. Family multiplicities are shared across paired methods, cases, and seeds within each source, preserving the frozen aggregation weights. It is a pooled interval, not an interval for every subgroup or for another comparator. The stored episode summaries do not contain row-level residuals; the paper preserves the supplied interval rather than reconstructing it from three seed averages. Seed markers in figures are descriptive variation, not confidence limits.\n   147\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#uncertainty]]\n   148\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.paired_gain_ci_low]]\n   149\t\n   150\t\\subsection{Historical-correspondence and state controls}\n   151\tEach source/split/seed condition is paired with a fit on independently deranged historical columns. The control preserves column marginals and missingness while disrupting historical cross-column row correspondence. Test scores remain unchanged. This tests sensitivity to the alignment available in historical scores, not an isolated acquisition component. A reset replay checks the initial actual condition for repeated legal queries and clipped predictions. Controls and replay have zero primary aggregation weight; replay is not another independent seed. The protocol retains original denominators for failures rather than silently dropping incomplete cases.\n   152\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#controls,aggregation]]\n   153\t\n   154\tThe retained method is the unchanged original reference implementation: the preceding selection process produced no measured search improvement, and its three alternative evaluations returned no usable scores, establishing neither comparative losses nor their failure causes. Independently, the final evaluation completed and met its recorded qualification criteria; this does not certify generalization beyond the stated protocol, as detailed in the supplement.\n   155\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.status]]\n   156\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n   157\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\n   158\t\n   159\t\\section{Results}\n   160\t\\subsection{Direct comparison of complete policies}\n   161\tTable~\\ref{tab:pooled} reports the pooled scores and their equivalent absolute errors. Variance LF scores $0.9528$, compared with $0.9364$ for Random LF, an observed difference of $0.01635$. The supplied paired interval is $[0.01481,0.01848]$. Within the frozen population and weighting, this supports a positive operational contrast for variance-guided versus random queries with the matched factor construction. It does not compare prediction errors on identical target panels.\n   162\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n   163\t\n   164\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   165\t\\centering\n   166\t\\input{tables/pooled}\n   167\t\\caption{Pooled prediction scores and normalized MAE. LF denotes masked low-rank reconstruction. All methods receive the same source/split/seed and budget protocol, but their purchased indices and scored remainders can differ. Only Variance LF versus Random LF has a supplied paired uncertainty interval.}\n   168\t\\label{tab:pooled}\n   169\t\\end{table}\n   170\t\n   171\tHistorical means already score $0.9069$, illustrating why a high absolute score alone is insufficient evidence of useful querying. Adding a model-level offset raises the score to $0.9206$. Medoid ridge reaches $0.9327$, while information-selected ridge reaches $0.9482$. Thus an informative covariance-based policy captures much of the observed predictability without the studied factor representation. Its pooled difference from Variance LF is $0.00454$; there is no supplied paired interval establishing statistical superiority against this stronger comparator.\n   172\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n   173\t\n   174\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   175\t\\centering\n   176\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_comparison.pdf}\n   177\t\\caption{Direct comparisons under (a) MTEB model separation, (b) MTEB name-family separation, (c) Open LLM model separation, and (d) Open LLM name-family separation. Small dots show the three final seeds; diamonds show their means. Horizontal positions share the same explicitly restricted score axis. No dots are uncertainty intervals. Variance LF has the highest mean in each panel; the individual-seed reversal against Info ridge remains visible in panel (d).}\n   178\t\\label{fig:main}\n   179\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   180\t\\end{figure}\n   181\t\n   182\t\\subsection{Source, split, and seed heterogeneity}\n   183\tFigure~\\ref{fig:main} retains all source/split conditions instead of displaying only the pooled ranking. Mean Variance LF scores are $0.9496$ and $0.9442$ on MTEB's model and name-family splits, and $0.9591$ and $0.9582$ on Open LLM's corresponding splits. The pooled source scores are $0.9469$ for MTEB and $0.9586$ for Open LLM. These are not controlled source-difficulty comparisons: the benchmark collections, availability patterns, and budget ranges differ.\n   184\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#conditions]]\n   185\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mteb_score]]\n   186\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.open_llm_score]]\n   187\t\n   188\tThe matched random-query factor baseline has lower observed scores in every retained source/split/seed cell. However, universal dominance across all comparators is not observed. On Open LLM's name-family split at seed $1019$, Variance LF scores $0.953104$, whereas Info ridge scores $0.953316$. Rounding these to fewer digits can hide the reversal. This small counterexample is important because the pooled ordering alone could suggest that the factor representation is always preferable. The evidence supports neither that conclusion nor isolated attribution to factorization rather than query selection.\n   189\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   190\t\n   191\tThe supplementary checkpoint curves show increasing observed scores with larger budgets along each retained trajectory. They cannot determine a universally sufficient $k$: there is no externally specified acceptable error threshold, no uncertainty interval by budget, and fewer available targets remain at later checkpoints. Nor do the summaries contain baseline checkpoint curves from which to infer budget-specific advantages over ridge. The central comparison is the prespecified average over checkpoint budgets.\n   192\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   193\t\n   194\t\\subsection{Sensitivity to historical correspondence}\n   195\tFigure~\\ref{fig:control} pairs the actual and deranged-history measurements. The pooled studied score falls from $0.9528$ to $0.9234$, an actual-minus-control gap of $0.02937$. Every retained source/split/seed pair has a lower observed score under the control. Useful historical structure therefore survives the source extraction and splitting procedures sufficiently to affect the measured policy. Benchmark marginals alone are not enough to reproduce its actual-history result.\n   196\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.corruption_gap]]\n   197\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permuted_score]]\n   198\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   199\t\n   200\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   201\t\\centering\n   202\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/historical_dependence_control.pdf}\n   203\t\\caption{Historical-correspondence control by source. Each line joins aligned and independently column-deranged histories for one retained seed and split; it does not depict temporal change. Color and marker distinguish split definitions. The control changes the learned representation and potentially the query set, so the score gap is not an acquisition-only effect.}\n   204\t\\label{fig:control}\n   205\t\\end{figure}\n   206\t\n   207\tThis is a dependence check, not proof that the low-rank model is true. The control perturbs factor learning, selection, and prediction jointly, and could also alter which targets remain. It cannot identify the best latent rank, verify that missingness is ignorable, or certify absence of benchmark contamination. All required final cases completed, and the reset replay passed; these facts support execution consistency within the tested interface, not comprehensive scientific validity.\n   208\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n   209\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reset_replay_ok]]\n   210\t\n   211\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   212\t\\paragraph{What the comparison identifies.}\n   213\tThe evidence is strongest for a policy-level statement: for these public score matrices, splits, and checkpoint weights, variance-guided factor reconstruction improves the observed remaining-target objective over its random-query counterpart. This is a useful decision problem when the evaluator cares precisely about whichever available benchmarks were not purchased. It is a weaker basis for a statement about reconstructing a prespecified panel, because the policy controls the scored target composition as well as the information collected. A fixed untouched panel, shared query sets for predictor ablations, and a selector-only comparison on that panel would separate these effects; none is reported here.\n   214\t\n   215\t\\paragraph{What the mechanism does not establish.}\n   216\tThe acquisition formula uses a conditional quadratic proxy with a unit-noise working interpretation. No empirical calibration of that uncertainty is available. The optimized proxy includes a coordinate that subsequently leaves the target set, and the final score uses absolute rather than squared loss. These mismatches do not invalidate the observed contrast, but they prevent interpreting it as validation of an optimality theorem. Likewise, availability-conditioned sequential choices should not be described as learning which question to ask from a new model's answers: the implemented choice ignores those answers.\n   217\t\n   218\t\\paragraph{Scope of the data distribution.}\n   219\tHistorical scores are selectively observed. Coverage filtering and the supplied availability mask restrict the population to models with enough measured benchmarks, potentially excluding the hardest prospective cases. Name-derived family labels can miss cross-namespace relationships and do not guarantee separation of pretraining data, fine-tuning lineages, or shared evaluation contamination. Historical-cell rank selection also does not measure sensitivity to tuning on entirely unseen families. The final splits and derangement control constrain some interpretations, but they do not justify arbitrary out-of-distribution or future-model generalization. Implementation interpretation uses the inspectable retained source; an independently verifiable byte-identical final snapshot is unavailable, as detailed in the supplement.\n   220\t\n   221\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty, costs, and practical use.}\n   222\tThe supplied bootstrap interval concerns one pooled matched-factor contrast under heuristic family clustering. It is not evidence of significance against all baselines; subgroup differences and the ridge comparison remain descriptive. The experiment purchases benchmark scores retrospectively from a frozen table and assumes equal query costs. It therefore demonstrates prediction under a score budget, not measured savings in inference time, energy, or monetary cost. A practical evaluator should select its own error tolerance, retain direct measurement of safety-critical or poorly represented benchmarks, and test a fixed target panel before relying on inferred scores for model release decisions.\n   223\t\n   224\t\\paragraph{What averaging hides.}\n   225\tEqual row weights give sparse and dense held-out models the same contribution after their target errors are averaged. Equal source weights likewise prevent the larger generative-model table from overwhelming the embedding-model setting. These are explicit priorities of the evaluation, not invariances of the underlying inference problem. A different deployment population, benchmark weighting, or cost model can change the policy ranking. Moreover, a small average absolute error can coexist with large errors on a particular capability. The retained summaries contain neither per-benchmark residual distributions nor tail-error intervals, so they do not support certifying individual benchmark predictions, identifying which tasks can safely be skipped, or converting the pooled score into a deployment-specific reliability threshold. Reporting the source/split views and the local ridge reversal is therefore necessary, but not a substitute for task-level validation when choosing an actual evaluation panel.\n   226\t\n   227\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   228\tHistorical leaderboard measurements can support budgeted reconstruction without fitting a large predictor or running new language-model inference. In the evaluated setting, a regularized masked factor model coupled to variance-guided queries yields a higher pooled remaining-target score than the same factor construction with random queries. The historical-column control further shows that useful correspondence across benchmarks contributes to the measured result. These observations hold across the separately fitted embedding-model and generative-model sources and their two split definitions, rather than establishing transfer of a common representation.\n   229\t\n   230\tThe most important qualification is the target itself. Query policies remove different coordinates from evaluation, and the strongest covariance comparator is competitive, including a retained local reversal. The measurements therefore do not establish fixed-target superiority, score-adaptive selection, calibrated uncertainty, or a universal number of sufficient benchmarks. The appropriate conclusion is narrower: variance-guided masked reconstruction is a useful operational policy in the observed regime, with clear reasons to require shared-target evaluation, stronger family definitions, and explicit deployment costs before making broader claims.\n   231\t\n   232\t\\clearpage\n   233\t\\bibliography{references}\n   234\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n   235\t\n   236\t\\clearpage\n   237\t\\appendix\n   238\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   239\t\\subsection{Inspectable implementation and analytic identity}\n   240\tThe retained evaluated implementation is supplied at\n   241\t\\path{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}; the original reference is at\n   242\t\\path{source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py}.\n   243\tTheir readable source contents match. The entrypoint contract is in the adjacent \\texttt{program\\_spec.toml} files. Factor fitting is at lines 45--62, rank selection at lines 65--83, acquisition at lines 114--127, and conditional prediction at lines 134--142. Comparison implementations are inspectable in \\path{source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py}, especially \\texttt{reference\\_fit}, \\texttt{run\\_baseline}, and \\texttt{curve\\_score}. These files, rather than source hashes alone, support the implementation description. The final manifest and the selection registry record different tree digests; no separate final source snapshot is supplied. We therefore do not certify byte-level identity of the final snapshot from hashes or method-family alignment alone. Implementation-level interpretation is conditional on the supplied retained-source association; final measurements and qualification are read independently from evaluator evidence.\n   244\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py]]\n   245\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py]]\n   246\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py]]\n   247\t\n   248\tFor completeness, set $H_Q=V_Q^\\top V_Q+\\lambda I$. Since $\\lambda>0$, $H_Q$ is invertible. Multiplying $H_Q+v_av_a^\\top$ by the right side of Equation~\\ref{eq:rankone} gives the identity matrix: the added rank-one terms cancel after division by $1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a$. Multiplying the covariance difference on the left by $V_T$ and on the right by $V_T^\\top$ and taking a trace gives\n   249\t\\[\n   250\t \\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left[V_T(C_Q-C_{Q\\cup\\{a\\}})V_T^\\top\\right]\n   251\t =\\frac{\\|V_TC_Qv_a\\|_2^2}{1+v_a^\\top C_Qv_a}.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
