{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '154,225p'; cat claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"   154\tThe retained method is the unchanged original reference implementation: the preceding selection process produced no measured search improvement, and its three alternative evaluations returned no usable scores, establishing neither comparative losses nor their failure causes. Independently, the final evaluation completed and met its recorded qualification criteria; this does not certify generalization beyond the stated protocol, as detailed in the supplement.\n   155\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.status]]\n   156\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n   157\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\n   158\t\n   159\t\\section{Results}\n   160\t\\subsection{Direct comparison of complete policies}\n   161\tTable~\\ref{tab:pooled} reports the pooled scores and their equivalent absolute errors. Variance LF scores $0.9528$, compared with $0.9364$ for Random LF, an observed difference of $0.01635$. The supplied paired interval is $[0.01481,0.01848]$. Within the frozen population and weighting, this supports a positive operational contrast for variance-guided versus random queries with the matched factor construction. It does not compare prediction errors on identical target panels.\n   162\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n   163\t\n   164\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   165\t\\centering\n   166\t\\input{tables/pooled}\n   167\t\\caption{Pooled prediction scores and normalized MAE. LF denotes masked low-rank reconstruction. All methods receive the same source/split/seed and budget protocol, but their purchased indices and scored remainders can differ. Only Variance LF versus Random LF has a supplied paired uncertainty interval.}\n   168\t\\label{tab:pooled}\n   169\t\\end{table}\n   170\t\n   171\tHistorical means already score $0.9069$, illustrating why a high absolute score alone is insufficient evidence of useful querying. Adding a model-level offset raises the score to $0.9206$. Medoid ridge reaches $0.9327$, while information-selected ridge reaches $0.9482$. Thus an informative covariance-based policy captures much of the observed predictability without the studied factor representation. Its pooled difference from Variance LF is $0.00454$; there is no supplied paired interval establishing statistical superiority against this stronger comparator.\n   172\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n   173\t\n   174\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   175\t\\centering\n   176\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_comparison.pdf}\n   177\t\\caption{Direct comparisons under (a) MTEB model separation, (b) MTEB name-family separation, (c) Open LLM model separation, and (d) Open LLM name-family separation. Small dots show the three final seeds; diamonds show their means. Horizontal positions share the same explicitly restricted score axis. No dots are uncertainty intervals. Variance LF has the highest mean in each panel; the individual-seed reversal against Info ridge remains visible in panel (d).}\n   178\t\\label{fig:main}\n   179\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   180\t\\end{figure}\n   181\t\n   182\t\\subsection{Source, split, and seed heterogeneity}\n   183\tFigure~\\ref{fig:main} retains all source/split conditions instead of displaying only the pooled ranking. Mean Variance LF scores are $0.9496$ and $0.9442$ on MTEB's model and name-family splits, and $0.9591$ and $0.9582$ on Open LLM's corresponding splits. The pooled source scores are $0.9469$ for MTEB and $0.9586$ for Open LLM. These are not controlled source-difficulty comparisons: the benchmark collections, availability patterns, and budget ranges differ.\n   184\t% @evidence[[derived_results.json#conditions]]\n   185\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mteb_score]]\n   186\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.open_llm_score]]\n   187\t\n   188\tThe matched random-query factor baseline has lower observed scores in every retained source/split/seed cell. However, universal dominance across all comparators is not observed. On Open LLM's name-family split at seed $1019$, Variance LF scores $0.953104$, whereas Info ridge scores $0.953316$. Rounding these to fewer digits can hide the reversal. This small counterexample is important because the pooled ordering alone could suggest that the factor representation is always preferable. The evidence supports neither that conclusion nor isolated attribution to factorization rather than query selection.\n   189\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   190\t\n   191\tThe supplementary checkpoint curves show increasing observed scores with larger budgets along each retained trajectory. They cannot determine a universally sufficient $k$: there is no externally specified acceptable error threshold, no uncertainty interval by budget, and fewer available targets remain at later checkpoints. Nor do the summaries contain baseline checkpoint curves from which to infer budget-specific advantages over ridge. The central comparison is the prespecified average over checkpoint budgets.\n   192\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   193\t\n   194\t\\subsection{Sensitivity to historical correspondence}\n   195\tFigure~\\ref{fig:control} pairs the actual and deranged-history measurements. The pooled studied score falls from $0.9528$ to $0.9234$, an actual-minus-control gap of $0.02937$. Every retained source/split/seed pair has a lower observed score under the control. Useful historical structure therefore survives the source extraction and splitting procedures sufficiently to affect the measured policy. Benchmark marginals alone are not enough to reproduce its actual-history result.\n   196\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.corruption_gap]]\n   197\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.permuted_score]]\n   198\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n   199\t\n   200\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   201\t\\centering\n   202\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/historical_dependence_control.pdf}\n   203\t\\caption{Historical-correspondence control by source. Each line joins aligned and independently column-deranged histories for one retained seed and split; it does not depict temporal change. Color and marker distinguish split definitions. The control changes the learned representation and potentially the query set, so the score gap is not an acquisition-only effect.}\n   204\t\\label{fig:control}\n   205\t\\end{figure}\n   206\t\n   207\tThis is a dependence check, not proof that the low-rank model is true. The control perturbs factor learning, selection, and prediction jointly, and could also alter which targets remain. It cannot identify the best latent rank, verify that missingness is ignorable, or certify absence of benchmark contamination. All required final cases completed, and the reset replay passed; these facts support execution consistency within the tested interface, not comprehensive scientific validity.\n   208\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n   209\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reset_replay_ok]]\n   210\t\n   211\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   212\t\\paragraph{What the comparison identifies.}\n   213\tThe evidence is strongest for a policy-level statement: for these public score matrices, splits, and checkpoint weights, variance-guided factor reconstruction improves the observed remaining-target objective over its random-query counterpart. This is a useful decision problem when the evaluator cares precisely about whichever available benchmarks were not purchased. It is a weaker basis for a statement about reconstructing a prespecified panel, because the policy controls the scored target composition as well as the information collected. A fixed untouched panel, shared query sets for predictor ablations, and a selector-only comparison on that panel would separate these effects; none is reported here.\n   214\t\n   215\t\\paragraph{What the mechanism does not establish.}\n   216\tThe acquisition formula uses a conditional quadratic proxy with a unit-noise working interpretation. No empirical calibration of that uncertainty is available. The optimized proxy includes a coordinate that subsequently leaves the target set, and the final score uses absolute rather than squared loss. These mismatches do not invalidate the observed contrast, but they prevent interpreting it as validation of an optimality theorem. Likewise, availability-conditioned sequential choices should not be described as learning which question to ask from a new model's answers: the implemented choice ignores those answers.\n   217\t\n   218\t\\paragraph{Scope of the data distribution.}\n   219\tHistorical scores are selectively observed. Coverage filtering and the supplied availability mask restrict the population to models with enough measured benchmarks, potentially excluding the hardest prospective cases. Name-derived family labels can miss cross-namespace relationships and do not guarantee separation of pretraining data, fine-tuning lineages, or shared evaluation contamination. Historical-cell rank selection also does not measure sensitivity to tuning on entirely unseen families. The final splits and derangement control constrain some interpretations, but they do not justify arbitrary out-of-distribution or future-model generalization. Implementation interpretation uses the inspectable retained source; an independently verifiable byte-identical final snapshot is unavailable, as detailed in the supplement.\n   220\t\n   221\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty, costs, and practical use.}\n   222\tThe supplied bootstrap interval concerns one pooled matched-factor contrast under heuristic family clustering. It is not evidence of significance against all baselines; subgroup differences and the ridge comparison remain descriptive. The experiment purchases benchmark scores retrospectively from a frozen table and assumes equal query costs. It therefore demonstrates prediction under a score budget, not measured savings in inference time, energy, or monetary cost. A practical evaluator should select its own error tolerance, retain direct measurement of safety-critical or poorly represented benchmarks, and test a fixed target panel before relying on inferred scores for model release decisions.\n   223\t\n   224\t\\paragraph{What averaging hides.}\n   225\tEqual row weights give sparse and dense held-out models the same contribution after their target errors are averaged. Equal source weights likewise prevent the larger generative-model table from overwhelming the embedding-model setting. These are explicit priorities of the evaluation, not invariances of the underlying inference problem. A different deployment population, benchmark weighting, or cost model can change the policy ranking. Moreover, a small average absolute error can coexist with large errors on a particular capability. The retained summaries contain neither per-benchmark residual distributions nor tail-error intervals, so they do not support certifying individual benchmark predictions, identifying which tasks can safely be skipped, or converting the pooled score into a deployment-specific reliability threshold. Reporting the source/split views and the local ridge reversal is therefore necessary, but not a substitute for task-level validation when choosing an actual evaluation panel.\n{\n  \"scope\": \"Final evaluation results are independent of search selection. Implementation descriptions use the inspectable retained source, without claiming byte-identical final-snapshot identity.\",\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"The final evaluation completed and qualified; qualification is not proof of broad generalization.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The retained original reference has zero measured search score change; three alternative evaluations are unscored and do not establish measured losses or failure causes.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.investigation_outcome\", \"study.search_score_delta\", \"study.attempts.total\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.unscored\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The pooled studied score is 0.9527636891 and equivalent normalized MAE is 0.0472363109; rounded values in the abstract, results, and pooled table are 0.9528 and 0.0472.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Random-query low-rank pooled score is 0.9364101694, equivalent MAE 0.0635898306; the studied-minus-random difference is 0.0163535197.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.random_lowrank_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The supplied pooled paired family-clustered interval against random-query factors is [0.0148071678, 0.0184780194], not an interval against ridge or for subgroups.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_low\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_high\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Information-selected ridge scores 0.9482262260 (MAE 0.0517737740), with descriptive studied-minus-ridge difference 0.0045374631 and no supplied paired significance interval.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.selected_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Medoid ridge scores 0.9327472830 (MAE 0.0672527170), mean offset 0.9206056682 (MAE 0.0793943318), and historical means 0.9068768878 (MAE 0.0931231122).\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.medoid_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.copy_score\", \"final.metric.constant_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The studied method has the highest pooled observed score among the six evaluated policies, without statistical superiority being established against all comparators.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.random_lowrank_score\", \"final.metric.selected_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.medoid_ridge_score\", \"final.metric.copy_score\", \"final.metric.constant_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Three final seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019; replay is excluded from primary means and figures.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#aggregation\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Figure 2, supplementary Table 2, and derived_results.json retain each of the twelve primary source/split/seed cells and six policies, using control=false and replay=false.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"build_paper_artifacts.py\", \"derived_results.json\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The studied policy has higher observed scores than random-query factors in all twelve retained primary cells and the highest three-seed mean in each of four source/split conditions.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"derived_results.json#conditions\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Studied condition means are 0.9496/0.9442 for MTEB model/family splits and 0.9591/0.9582 for Open LLM model/family splits, rounded from saved observations.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"derived_results.json#conditions\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pooled source scores are 0.9468974985 for MTEB and 0.9586298797 for Open LLM; they do not constitute controlled source-difficulty comparisons.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.mteb_score\", \"final.metric.open_llm_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Open LLM family split at seed 1019 gives studied score 0.9531041725 versus information-selected ridge 0.9533160600, a retained local reversal.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes[2].solution.cases\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pooled independently deranged-history score is 0.9233917542 and actual-minus-control gap is 0.0293719349.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.permuted_score\", \"final.metric.corruption_gap\", \"final.metric.combined_score\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Every retained source/split/seed pair has a lower observed studied-policy score under historical-column derangement, as shown in Figure 3 and supplementary Table 3; the contrast changes learning, querying, and target composition jointly.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.corruption_gap\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#permute_training\", \"tables/controls.tex\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The final required cases completed and reset replay passed; these are interface/execution controls, not comprehensive validity guarantees.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.failed_episode_fraction\", \"final.metric.control_failure_fraction\", \"final.metric.reset_replay_ok\", \"final.qualification.status\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Supplementary Figure 4 shows only the studied-policy measured checkpoint arrays, increasing in each retained actual trajectory; no baseline checkpoint comparison, fixed-panel curve, or universally sufficient budget is inferred.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"Recorded seed durations are 24.0816306723, 22.8890082091, 23.0760153015 seconds, excluding independent acquisition; no measured inference-cost, end-to-end storage, or speed advantage is asserted.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": []},\n    {\"claim\": \"The inspectable retained source implements masked alternating ridge factors, fixed observed column means, and SVD initialization; lambda=0.1, candidate ranks 1/2/4/8, seeded 10% cell validation, at most four validation sweeps and eight refit sweeps capped by requested steps.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl\", \"Koren\", \"Jain\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L45-L83\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Equations 1 and 3 describe clipped remaining-target 1-MAE and context-independent ridge latent prediction; availability is known and historical missing cells are masked, not observed zeros.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#curve_score\", \"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L110-L142\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The retained-source acquisition in Equation 4 uses a unit-noise quadratic trace-reduction proxy, includes the queried coordinate, and ignores purchased score values. Equation 5 is a fixed-target matrix identity, not an MAE optimality theorem.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Chaloner\", \"Cohn\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L114-L127\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Random LF uses matching reference factor fit/prediction construction; the ridge comparators use zero-filled centered covariance and penalties 0.01/0.1/1 chosen with every-fifth-row historical validation; means and mean-offset policies use random legal queries.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#reference_fit,predict_factors,run_baseline\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The readable retained and original reference files match, but exact byte-level identity to the separately hashed final snapshot is not independently certified; mechanism interpretation is conditional on supplied-source association.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.selection_kind\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\", \"source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The evaluated source regimes are separately fitted MTEB embedding-model scores and Open LLM six raw generative benchmark columns; no learned cross-source transfer is asserted.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MTEB\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#sources,extraction\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#build_case\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Pinned MTEB revision is 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383; pinned Open LLM revision is 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2. MTEB extraction uses natural-bound original test scores and default/English task/revision/subset columns; Open LLM uses raw scores. The latter dataset license is undeclared.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MTEB\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#sources,extraction\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The inspected final protocol uses model and heuristic name-family disjoint groups, approximately 70% historical groups, twenty-observation MTEB column support, and coverage filters; family labels are not authoritative lineage.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#split_components,require_disjoint_rows,build_case\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/audit.json#extraction\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"MTEB checkpoints are 1/2/4/8, Open LLM checkpoints 1/2/3. Target, row, checkpoint, seed, split, source weighting is nested and equal at each level; control/replay have zero primary weight.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#aggregation,production\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"The supplied interval uses 200 paired family-clustered bootstrap draws with RNG24681357 and 2.5/97.5 percentile endpoints, shared family multiplicities across paired policies, cases and seeds within source. It is preserved, not reconstructed from seed means.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_low\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_ci_high\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Efron\", \"Owen\"], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#uncertainty\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Documented evaluation versions are CPython3.12.15, NumPy1.26.4, SciPy1.12.0, PyArrow16.1.0, with CPU operation, isolation and broker requirements; this is not a newly measured cross-platform reproduction.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#runtime\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#RPC\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"No fixed untouched target panel, response-adaptive selector, controlled nonlinear superiority, universal budget sufficiency, or family-held-out tuning is supported. Diagnostic descriptions remain unverified.\", \"source_ids\": [\"study.attempts.unscored\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"], \"citation_keys\": [], \"evidence_refs\": [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/plan.json#limitations\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"MMLU, MTEB and HELM motivate heterogeneous evaluation coverage; item-level compression in tinyBenchmarks and metabench differs from buying aggregated benchmark scores; BenchBench motivates care about agreement methodology.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.MMLU\", \"ref.allowed.MTEB\", \"ref.allowed.HELM\", \"ref.allowed.Tiny\", \"ref.allowed.Metabench\", \"ref.allowed.BenchBench\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"MMLU\", \"MTEB\", \"HELM\", \"Tiny\", \"Metabench\", \"BenchBench\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Observational scaling laws and Sloth motivate low-dimensional multi-benchmark capability descriptions, without establishing interpreted skill recovery or scaling-law estimation by this study.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.Observational\", \"ref.allowed.Sloth\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Observational\", \"Sloth\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Matrix factorization, probabilistic/Bayesian factor models, and implicit-feedback models provide distinct methodological precedents; this study fits regularized numerical-score coordinates rather than a fully Bayesian or implicit-preference model.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.Koren\", \"ref.allowed.PMF\", \"ref.allowed.BayesianMF\", \"ref.allowed.Hu\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Koren\", \"PMF\", \"BayesianMF\", \"Hu\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Convex, spectral, and alternating matrix completion methods have assumption-dependent guarantees that are not verified for the selectively observed matrices here.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.CandesRecht\", \"ref.allowed.CandesTao\", \"ref.allowed.Keshavan\", \"ref.allowed.Mazumder\", \"ref.allowed.Jain\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"CandesRecht\", \"CandesTao\", \"Keshavan\", \"Mazumder\", \"Jain\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Ridge regularization, Bayesian design and statistical active learning are methodological context for the inspected solves and proxy, not evidence of novel architecture or universal MAE optimality.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.Hoerl\", \"ref.allowed.Chaloner\", \"ref.allowed.Cohn\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Hoerl\", \"Chaloner\", \"Cohn\"]},\n    {\"claim\": \"Missing-data assumptions and incomplete-data likelihood methods are separate from merely optimizing a masked loss; generic bootstrap and dependent-array resampling motivate specifying the resampling unit.\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.allowed.Rubin\", \"ref.allowed.Schafer\", \"ref.allowed.Dempster\", \"ref.allowed.Efron\", \"ref.allowed.Owen\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Rubin\", \"Schafer\", \"Dempster\", \"Efron\", \"Owen\"]}\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
